  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "A game show uses a roulette wheel divided into $50$ panels. There are $15$ blue, $15$ green, $15$ yellow, $2$ black, and $3$ white panels randomly shuffled on the roulette wheel. What is the probability that the roulette wheel stops on a white or green panel?",
  "worked_solution": "Let $W$ be the event that the roulette will stop on a white panel, and let $G$ be the event that the roulette will stop on a green panel. Then the probability that the roulette stops on a white or green panel given by $P(W\\cup G).$\n\nGiven that the roulette will stop at only one panel, the events $W$ and $G$  are mutually exclusive, i.e., $P(W\\cap G)=0.$ \n\nWe can find $P(W)$ and $P(G)$ by computing the necessary ratios. There are $50$ panels in the roulette. Of those panels, $3$ are white, and $15$ are green. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =\\frac{3}{50}. \\\\ P(G) & =\\frac{15}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W\\cup G) & =P(W)+P(G)-P(W\\cap G) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{50}+\\frac{15}{50}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
